      "bigPicture": "<p>A <b>healthcare system</b> is far more than a hospital. It is every person, institution, payment arrangement, and resource that delivers and manages care—locally, statewide, nationally, and internationally. As a nurse you work inside this system every shift, and many of your most important decisions are really system decisions: Is this the right setting for this client? Is this teaching preventing disease, catching it early, or helping someone live with it? What in this client’s life—money, housing, transportation, reading ability—will keep the plan from working once they leave?</p><p>The NCLEX rarely asks you to recite a definition of “secondary prevention.” Instead it describes a nurse doing something—teaching a testicular self-exam, referring a client with a new urostomy, giving a hepatitis B vaccine—and asks which level of prevention that represents, or which action best fits a community’s needs. The trick is to ask one question every time: <em>Does this client already have the disease or problem?</em> If not, it is primary. If it is present but early or not yet causing complications, the action is secondary. If it is established and the goal is restoring function or quality of life, it is tertiary.</p><p>The second half of this topic explains why two clients with the same diagnosis get very different outcomes. Social determinants of health, adverse childhood experiences, an aging and more diverse population, new technology, and health literacy all decide whether excellent care on paper becomes excellent care in real life. Exam items reward the nurse who notices these cues—a client who “forgot my glasses” whenever asked to read, a family with no car, a teen with a history of household violence—and responds with assessment, plain-language teaching, and the right referral rather than judgment.</p>",
