          "heading": "North Carolina practice note",
          "body": "<p><b>ACEs and mandatory reporting.</b> North Carolina is a <b>universal reporting</b> state. Under G.S. 7B-301, <em>any</em> person or institution with cause to suspect that a juvenile (under 18) is abused, neglected, or dependent, or has died from maltreatment, must report to the county department of social services (DSS) director. The nurse reports the suspicion—not proof—and does not investigate or wait for confirmation. Knowingly failing to report is a crime, and good-faith reporters are immune from liability. This aligns with the national NCLEX standard: suspected child maltreatment is reported to the designated child protective agency.</p><p><b>Vulnerable adults.</b> Under G.S. 108A-102, any person with reasonable cause to believe a disabled adult is in need of protective services must report to the county DSS director, orally or in writing; good-faith reporters are immune. Neglect, exploitation, and unsafe living conditions uncovered during an SDOH assessment of a disabled adult can trigger this duty.</p><p><b>Telehealth across state lines.</b> North Carolina participates in the Nurse Licensure Compact, so an RN with a multistate license whose primary state of residence is North Carolina may practice in other compact states, including by telehealth, while following the nursing practice laws of the state where the client is located. Nursing practice in NC is regulated by the North Carolina Board of Nursing under the Nursing Practice Act (G.S. 90-171.19 et seq.).</p>"
