          "heading": "How the NCLEX tests this: access, insurance, and referral decisions",
          "body": "<p>Common patterns: <b>(1) Best response</b> to a client facing a cost or access barrier; <b>(2) referral selection</b>—which client should the nurse refer to the case manager, or which program fits a family; <b>(3) EMTALA</b>—what the ED must do for a client who cannot pay; <b>(4) teaching</b> about appropriate use of primary, urgent, and emergency care. Sample question: <em>“A parent brings a 4-year-old to the emergency department for ear pain that began this evening. The parent gave acetaminophen 15 minutes before arrival. The child is afebrile and playing. The parent says, ‘We don’t have a regular doctor.’ Which action by the nurse is most appropriate after the child is screened and found stable?”</em> A. Tell the parent the ED is for emergencies only and the child should have been kept home. B. Explain how long acetaminophen takes to work and help the family establish care with a primary care provider. C. Recommend that the parent give a second dose of acetaminophen now to speed relief. D. Advise the parent to use urgent care for all future illnesses instead of the ED.</p>",
