          "heading": "North Carolina practice note",
          "body": "<p><b>Minors’ access to care.</b> Access barriers include confidentiality fears for adolescents. Under G.S. 90-21.5, a minor in North Carolina may give effective consent for services for (1) prevention, diagnosis, and treatment of venereal/sexually transmitted diseases and other reportable diseases; (2) pregnancy (prevention, diagnosis, and treatment)—but <em>not</em> sterilization or abortion; (3) abuse of controlled substances or alcohol; and (4) emotional disturbance. Emancipated minors may consent to their own care, and the age of majority is 18. Knowing this lets the nurse help a teen enter care for these services rather than turning the teen away for lack of a parent.</p><p><b>Vulnerable adults and access.</b> When an access assessment reveals that a disabled adult is being neglected, exploited, or is unable to obtain essential care and needs protective services, G.S. 108A-102 requires any person with reasonable cause to believe this to report to the county DSS director; good-faith reporters are immune.</p><p><b>Telehealth and licensure.</b> North Carolina is a Nurse Licensure Compact state, which supports nurses providing telehealth and case management across compact state lines with a multistate license. Practice is governed by the NC Board of Nursing under the NC Nursing Practice Act (G.S. 90-171.19 et seq.).</p>"
