      "bigPicture": "<p>Every unit you work on has a <b>nursing care delivery model</b>—a plan for who cares for which clients and how work is divided. Sometimes it is written in policy; sometimes you notice it only when it changes, such as during a snowstorm when half the staff cannot get in and the charge nurse says, “You pass all the meds, you do all the dressings, and the aides do baths.” That is functional nursing, and it keeps tasks moving at the cost of fragmented, depersonalized care. On a normal day you might be the RN team leader with an LPN and a nursing assistant, or a primary nurse who follows a client from admission to discharge.</p><p>Why does this matter on the NCLEX? Because delivery models are the setting for the exam’s favorite management questions: <em>Which task can the RN delegate to the UAP? Which client should be assigned to the LPN? What is the team leader’s responsibility when a task goes wrong?</em> The underlying rule never changes: the RN can delegate tasks but retains accountability, and assessment, teaching, evaluation, and care of unstable clients stay with the RN.</p><p>The last part of this topic zooms out. U.S. healthcare spending is the highest in the world, and resources—ICU beds, staff, ventilators, organs, money—are finite. <b>Resource allocation</b> and <b>rationing</b> are the decisions about who gets what. Nurses make micro-level allocation decisions every shift (who gets the one sitter, which client is seen first) and influence macro-level policy. The nurse’s ethical role is to advocate for clients, especially vulnerable ones, to use resources wisely, and to make fair, transparent, needs-based decisions.</p>",
