          "heading": "Course source & clinical clarification",
          "body": "<p>Module 46 · Healthcare Systems study guide, outcomes 46.5–46.7; textbook pp. 2780–2782 (functional, team, and primary nursing; UAP; delivery-model comparison table; EBP on decentralized workstations; drivers of rising costs; concepts related to healthcare systems; introduction to resource allocation). The study guide notes that p. 2782 was partially illegible and that full definitions of resource allocation and rationing appear on p. 2783; the definitions here (macro/microallocation, explicit/implicit rationing, distributive justice) are standard nursing-ethics content added to fill that gap.</p><p><b>Clarifications:</b> (1) The $10,739 per-capita figure corresponds to 2017 U.S. national health expenditure data; CMS figures for more recent years exceed $14,000 per person. Hospital care remains the largest share. (2) Functional nursing is coordinated by the charge (head) nurse, who technically <em>assigns</em> tasks within each person’s scope and <em>delegates</em> to UAP. (3) Total patient care and the five rights of delegation were added as standard NCLEX content. (4) “Team nursing is the most frequently used model” is retained from the course; many hospitals use hybrids of team nursing and total patient care. (5) The course says the team leader “delegates” to RNs, LPNs, and UAP. Under the NCSBN/ANA National Guidelines for Nursing Delegation, giving another RN or an LPN work that is already within that person’s scope is technically an <b>assignment</b>; <b>delegation</b> is allowing someone (usually UAP) to perform a task beyond their usual role. Either way, the RN team leader remains accountable for the team’s clients.</p>"
